<script>
const BANK=[{"id":1,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"During skeletal muscle contraction, calcium binds to which regulatory protein?","choices":["Troponin","Myosin","Titin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Troponin binds calcium and helps move tropomyosin away from actin binding sites.","Myosin forms thick filaments and interacts with actin during contraction.","Titin is an elastic protein that helps stabilize the sarcomere."]},{"id":2,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"A muscle fiber has an outer membrane that conducts an action potential deep into the cell. Which structure performs this role?","choices":["Sarcolemma","Sarcoplasm","Sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber and conducts electrical signals.","Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of the muscle fiber.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and releases calcium."]},{"id":3,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which connective tissue layer surrounds an entire skeletal muscle and helps transmit force toward its tendon?","choices":["Epimysium","Perimysium","Endomysium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The epimysium surrounds the entire muscle.","The perimysium surrounds individual fascicles.","The endomysium surrounds individual muscle fibers."]},{"id":4,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which connective tissue layer surrounds a bundle of skeletal muscle fibers called a fascicle?","choices":["Perimysium","Epimysium","Endomysium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The perimysium surrounds a fascicle, which contains multiple muscle fibers.","The epimysium surrounds the entire muscle.","The endomysium surrounds an individual muscle fiber."]},{"id":5,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which connective tissue layer directly surrounds an individual skeletal muscle fiber?","choices":["Endomysium","Perimysium","Epimysium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The endomysium surrounds each individual muscle fiber.","The perimysium surrounds a fascicle.","The epimysium surrounds the whole muscle."]},{"id":6,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"A skeletal muscle fiber cannot release enough calcium because its intracellular calcium storage system is damaged. Which structure is primarily affected?","choices":["Sarcoplasmic reticulum","Sarcolemma","Endomysium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and releases calcium needed for contraction.","The sarcolemma is the muscle fiber membrane.","The endomysium is connective tissue surrounding muscle fibers."]},{"id":7,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which proteins interact directly to produce sliding of the thin filaments during skeletal muscle contraction?","choices":["Actin and myosin","Collagen and elastin","Troponin and calcium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Actin and myosin interact during the cross-bridge cycle to produce contraction.","These are connective tissue proteins and do not produce the actin-myosin sliding mechanism.","Calcium binds troponin, but these do not form the sliding filaments."]},{"id":8,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"The functional contractile unit between two Z discs in a skeletal muscle fiber is the:","choices":["Sarcomere","Fascicle","Myofibril"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A sarcomere extends from one Z disc to the next and is the basic contractile unit.","A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers.","A myofibril contains many repeating sarcomeres."]},{"id":9,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which muscle type is striated, involuntary, and specialized for pumping blood?","choices":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Cardiac muscle is striated, involuntary, and forms the contractile tissue of the heart.","Smooth muscle is involuntary but lacks the striations seen in cardiac muscle.","Skeletal muscle is striated but is generally under voluntary control."]},{"id":10,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which muscle type is best suited for involuntary movement of material through the digestive tract?","choices":["Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Smooth muscle contracts involuntarily in the walls of hollow organs such as the digestive tract.","Cardiac muscle is specialized for the heart.","Skeletal muscle primarily produces voluntary body movement."]},{"id":11,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"A patient cannot voluntarily contract a skeletal muscle because the motor neuron cannot release its neurotransmitter at the muscle. Which location is primarily affected?","choices":["Neuromuscular junction","Sarcomere","Tendon"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The neuromuscular junction is where the motor neuron communicates with the skeletal muscle fiber.","The sarcomere is the contractile unit of the muscle fiber.","A tendon connects muscle to bone and is not the neural communication site."]},{"id":12,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"What immediate event allows myosin heads to interact with actin during skeletal muscle contraction?","choices":["Calcium exposes binding sites on actin","ATP permanently locks myosin to actin","Sodium destroys tropomyosin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Calcium binds troponin and causes tropomyosin to move away from actin binding sites.","ATP is required for the cross-bridge cycle and does not permanently lock myosin to actin.","Sodium does not remove tropomyosin from actin."]},{"id":13,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which molecule provides the energy needed for the myosin cross-bridge cycle?","choices":["ATP","DNA","Hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["ATP supplies the energy needed for myosin movement and cycling.","DNA stores genetic information and is not the immediate energy source for contraction.","Hemoglobin transports oxygen and does not directly power the cross-bridge cycle."]},{"id":14,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"If calcium is removed from the sarcoplasm of a skeletal muscle fiber, what normally happens to actin-myosin interaction?","choices":["It decreases because binding sites become covered again","It permanently increases","It becomes independent of ATP"],"answer":0,"explanations":["When calcium falls, tropomyosin moves back over the binding sites on actin.","Low calcium promotes relaxation rather than increasing contraction.","The cross-bridge cycle still requires ATP."]},{"id":15,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which characteristic distinguishes cardiac muscle from skeletal muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle cells commonly have intercalated discs","Cardiac muscle is voluntary","Cardiac muscle lacks actin and myosin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Intercalated discs connect cardiac muscle cells and help coordinate contraction.","Cardiac muscle is involuntary.","Cardiac muscle contains actin and myosin."]},{"id":16,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"A biopsy shows elongated, striated, multinucleated fibers under voluntary control. Which tissue is most likely present?","choices":["Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Skeletal muscle fibers are striated, multinucleated, and generally voluntary.","Cardiac muscle is involuntary and usually has one central nucleus.","Smooth muscle is nonstriated and involuntary."]},{"id":17,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which structure contains many repeating contractile units called sarcomeres?","choices":["Myofibrils","Periosteum","Medullary cavity"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Myofibrils contain many repeating sarcomeres within a muscle fiber.","Periosteum covers the external surface of bone.","The medullary cavity is located within many long bones."]},{"id":18,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"If tropomyosin remains covering the actin binding sites even after calcium is released, what process is directly impaired?","choices":["Cross-bridge formation","ATP production in the liver","Bone remodeling"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Myosin cannot effectively attach to actin if the binding sites remain blocked.","ATP production is unrelated to the immediate problem described.","Bone remodeling is unrelated to actin-myosin interaction."]},{"id":19,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Which muscle type usually has spindle-shaped cells without obvious striations?","choices":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Smooth muscle cells are generally spindle-shaped and nonstriated.","Skeletal muscle fibers are long and striated.","Cardiac muscle is striated and branched."]},{"id":20,"topic":"Muscular System","q":"Why can skeletal muscle generate repeated contractions when ATP and calcium remain available?","choices":["Myosin heads repeatedly attach, pull, detach, and reset","Actin permanently disappears after contraction","Calcium becomes permanently attached to troponin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The cross-bridge cycle can repeat as long as the required conditions remain available.","Actin remains part of the thin filament.","Calcium binding is regulated and reversible."]},{"id":21,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which bone cell is primarily responsible for producing new bone matrix?","choices":["Osteoblast","Osteoclast","Osteocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Osteoblasts synthesize and deposit new bone matrix.","Osteoclasts break down bone matrix.","Osteocytes maintain mature bone tissue."]},{"id":22,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which bone cell breaks down bone tissue during bone remodeling?","choices":["Osteoclast","Osteoblast","Osteocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Osteoclasts resorb bone tissue during remodeling.","Osteoblasts build new bone matrix.","Osteocytes maintain bone matrix."]},{"id":23,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Mature bone cells that maintain the surrounding bone matrix are called:","choices":["Osteocytes","Osteoblasts","Osteoclasts"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Osteocytes are mature bone cells that maintain the surrounding matrix.","Osteoblasts produce new bone matrix.","Osteoclasts resorb bone."]},{"id":24,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which microscopic structure is characteristic of compact bone and contains concentric lamellae around a central canal?","choices":["Osteon","Trabecula","Epiphyseal plate"],"answer":0,"explanations":["An osteon is the basic structural unit of compact bone and contains concentric lamellae.","A trabecula is a supporting structure in spongy bone.","The epiphyseal plate is associated with bone growth."]},{"id":25,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which structure forms the lattice-like framework of spongy bone?","choices":["Trabeculae","Osteons","Central canals"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Trabeculae form the supportive lattice of spongy bone.","Osteons are characteristic structural units of compact bone.","Central canals are located within osteons."]},{"id":26,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which part of an osteon contains blood vessels and nerves that supply nearby bone cells?","choices":["Central canal","Lacuna","Canaliculus"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The central canal contains vessels and nerves within an osteon.","A lacuna houses an osteocyte.","Canaliculi connect lacunae."]},{"id":27,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"A patient has reduced blood calcium. Which hormone normally promotes processes that increase blood calcium concentration?","choices":["Parathyroid hormone","Insulin","Calcitonin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Parathyroid hormone helps raise blood calcium concentration.","Insulin primarily regulates glucose metabolism.","Calcitonin generally acts to decrease blood calcium."]},{"id":28,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Why is bone considered an important mineral reservoir?","choices":["It stores large amounts of calcium and phosphate","It stores most body glucose","It produces all plasma proteins"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Bone stores large amounts of calcium and phosphate that can participate in mineral regulation.","Glucose is not primarily stored in bone.","Bone does not produce all plasma proteins."]},{"id":29,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which process removes old or damaged bone as part of normal remodeling?","choices":["Bone resorption by osteoclasts","Bone deposition by osteocytes","Cartilage formation by neurons"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Osteoclasts remove bone tissue during the resorption phase of remodeling.","Osteocytes primarily maintain bone rather than perform major bone resorption.","Neurons do not perform bone remodeling."]},{"id":30,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which region of a long bone contains the growth plate in a growing individual?","choices":["Epiphysis/metaphyseal region","Medullary cavity only","Diaphysis center only"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The epiphyseal plate is located near the epiphysis and metaphysis during growth.","The medullary cavity is the central cavity and is not the growth plate.","The growth plate is not located in the center of the diaphysis."]},{"id":31,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which structure is a tough connective tissue covering on the external surface of most bones?","choices":["Periosteum","Endosteum","Articular cartilage"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The periosteum covers most external bone surfaces and contains connective tissue, vessels, and nerves.","The endosteum lines internal bone surfaces.","Articular cartilage covers joint surfaces."]},{"id":32,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which cavity of a long bone contains yellow marrow in many adults?","choices":["Medullary cavity","Central canal","Synovial cavity"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The medullary cavity is the central cavity of a long bone and may contain yellow marrow in adults.","The central canal belongs to an osteon.","A synovial cavity is associated with a joint."]},{"id":33,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Red bone marrow is especially important because it is a major site of:","choices":["Blood cell formation","Calcium digestion","Muscle contraction"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Red marrow contains hematopoietic tissue that produces blood cells.","Bone marrow does not digest calcium.","Muscle contraction occurs in muscle tissue."]},{"id":34,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"A fracture damages the dense outer region of a long bone. Which tissue is primarily involved?","choices":["Compact bone","Spongy bone","Cartilage only"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Compact bone forms the dense outer region of most bones.","Spongy bone is mainly internal and contains trabeculae.","Cartilage does not form the dense outer shaft of most long bones."]},{"id":35,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which bone cell would be expected to become more active when old bone needs to be removed?","choices":["Osteoclast","Osteoblast","Osteocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Osteoclasts are specialized for bone resorption.","Osteoblasts build new bone.","Osteocytes maintain bone tissue."]},{"id":36,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which bone cell would be expected to increase activity when new bone matrix needs to be deposited?","choices":["Osteoblast","Osteoclast","Osteocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Osteoblasts produce and deposit new bone matrix.","Osteoclasts resorb bone.","Osteocytes maintain mature bone tissue."]},{"id":37,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which structure allows nutrients and signals to travel between osteocytes through tiny channels?","choices":["Canaliculi","Osteons","Periosteum"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Canaliculi connect lacunae and allow communication between osteocytes.","Osteons are structural units of compact bone.","The periosteum is an external bone covering."]},{"id":38,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which major bone protects the brain within the cranial cavity?","choices":["Cranium","Scapula","Femur"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The cranium forms the protective bony case surrounding the brain.","The scapula is the shoulder blade.","The femur is the thigh bone."]},{"id":39,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"A person falls on an outstretched hand and fractures a bone of the forearm on the thumb side. Which bone is most likely involved?","choices":["Radius","Ulna","Humerus"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The radius is located on the thumb side of the forearm.","The ulna is on the little-finger side of the forearm.","The humerus is the upper arm bone."]},{"id":40,"topic":"Skeletal System","q":"Which process allows bone tissue to adapt to mechanical stress by continually removing and replacing matrix?","choices":["Bone remodeling","Hemopoiesis","Synaptic transmission"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Bone remodeling involves coordinated resorption and formation of bone.","Hemopoiesis is blood cell formation.","Synaptic transmission is communication between neurons or target cells."]},{"id":41,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Deoxygenated blood returning from the systemic circulation first enters which heart chamber?","choices":["Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The right atrium receives systemic venous blood through the venae cavae.","The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium."]},{"id":42,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which chamber pumps deoxygenated blood toward the lungs?","choices":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Right atrium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary circulation.","The left ventricle pumps blood into systemic circulation.","The right atrium receives returning venous blood."]},{"id":43,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which chamber receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs?","choices":["Left atrium","Right atrium","Left ventricle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood to the left atrium.","The right atrium receives systemic venous blood.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium."]},{"id":44,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which chamber generates the highest pressure because it pumps blood through systemic circulation?","choices":["Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Left atrium"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The left ventricle must generate high pressure to move blood through the entire systemic circuit.","The right ventricle pumps only through the lower-pressure pulmonary circuit.","The atria generate much less pressure than the ventricles."]},{"id":45,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessel carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the systemic circulation?","choices":["Aorta","Pulmonary artery","Superior vena cava"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The aorta receives blood directly from the left ventricle and distributes it systemically.","The pulmonary artery carries blood toward the lungs.","The superior vena cava returns systemic venous blood to the heart."]},{"id":46,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessels return systemic venous blood to the right atrium?","choices":["Superior and inferior venae cavae","Pulmonary veins","Pulmonary arteries"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The venae cavae return blood from the systemic circulation to the right atrium.","Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs.","Pulmonary arteries carry blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs."]},{"id":47,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessel carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs?","choices":["Pulmonary artery","Aorta","Pulmonary vein"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood into pulmonary circulation.","The aorta carries oxygenated systemic blood away from the left ventricle.","Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood to the left atrium."]},{"id":48,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessels carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium?","choices":["Pulmonary veins","Pulmonary arteries","Venae cavae"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.","Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood toward the lungs.","The venae cavae return systemic venous blood to the right atrium."]},{"id":49,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessel layer is closest to the blood within an artery or vein?","choices":["Tunica intima","Tunica media","Tunica externa"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The tunica intima is the innermost layer and directly contacts the blood.","The tunica media is the middle muscular layer.","The tunica externa is the outer layer."]},{"id":50,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessel layer contains substantial smooth muscle and helps regulate vessel diameter?","choices":["Tunica media","Tunica intima","Tunica externa"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The tunica media contains smooth muscle that contributes to vasoconstriction and vasodilation.","The tunica intima lines the vessel lumen.","The tunica externa is the outer connective tissue layer."]},{"id":51,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessels are especially effective at absorbing the pressure generated by ventricular contraction because of abundant elastic tissue?","choices":["Elastic arteries","Capillaries","Venules"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Elastic arteries contain abundant elastic tissue that helps accommodate pressure changes.","Capillaries are thin exchange vessels and do not contain large elastic layers.","Venules collect blood from capillary beds."]},{"id":52,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Why do many veins contain valves?","choices":["To reduce backward flow of blood","To increase oxygen production","To create red blood cells"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Valves help keep venous blood moving toward the heart and reduce backflow.","Valves do not produce oxygen.","Valves do not produce blood cells."]},{"id":53,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which vessel type is the primary site of exchange of gases, nutrients, and wastes between blood and tissues?","choices":["Capillary","Elastic artery","Large vein"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Capillaries have very thin walls specialized for exchange.","Elastic arteries mainly conduct blood under pressure.","Large veins primarily return blood toward the heart."]},{"id":54,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which blood component is primarily responsible for transporting oxygen using hemoglobin?","choices":["Red blood cells","Platelets","Basophils"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Red blood cells contain hemoglobin, which binds and transports oxygen.","Platelets are involved in hemostasis.","Basophils are white blood cells involved in immune responses."]},{"id":55,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which blood component is most directly involved in forming a platelet plug during vessel injury?","choices":["Platelets","Erythrocytes","Albumin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Platelets adhere to damaged vessel surfaces and participate in forming a platelet plug.","Red blood cells primarily transport gases.","Albumin is a plasma protein involved in maintaining oncotic pressure."]},{"id":56,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"A patient has an acute bacterial infection. Which leukocyte is typically one of the earliest and most abundant responders?","choices":["Neutrophil","Eosinophil","Basophil"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Neutrophils are abundant innate immune cells and commonly respond early to bacterial infection.","Eosinophils are more strongly associated with parasites and allergies.","Basophils are involved in allergic and inflammatory responses."]},{"id":57,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which leukocyte is strongly associated with allergic reactions and release of histamine?","choices":["Basophil","Neutrophil","Erythrocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Basophils can release histamine and participate in allergic responses.","Neutrophils commonly respond to bacterial infection.","Erythrocytes transport oxygen and are not leukocytes."]},{"id":58,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which leukocyte is particularly associated with parasitic infections and allergic responses?","choices":["Eosinophil","Neutrophil","Platelet"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Eosinophils are associated with parasitic infections and allergic responses.","Neutrophils are common early responders to bacterial infection.","Platelets are cell fragments involved in hemostasis."]},{"id":59,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"A person suddenly loses blood pressure. Which response helps maintain circulation by increasing heart rate and vascular tone?","choices":["Sympathetic activation","Decreased sympathetic activity","Immediate destruction of platelets"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Sympathetic activity increases heart rate and vascular tone to help compensate for low blood pressure.","Decreased sympathetic activity would generally reduce the compensatory response.","Platelet destruction does not compensate for low blood pressure."]},{"id":60,"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which sequence correctly represents normal systemic blood flow after blood leaves the left ventricle?","choices":["Aorta -> arteries -> capillaries -> veins -> venae cavae","Venae cavae -> arteries -> capillaries -> aorta","Pulmonary artery -> veins -> aorta -> capillaries"],"answer":0,"explanations":["This sequence represents blood leaving the left ventricle and eventually returning through systemic veins.","The venae cavae return blood to the right atrium and do not begin systemic arterial flow.","This sequence does not represent normal systemic circulation."]},{"id":61,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which two structures make up the central nervous system?","choices":["Brain and spinal cord","Cranial nerves and spinal nerves","Ganglia and receptors"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The brain and spinal cord together form the central nervous system.","These are part of the peripheral nervous system.","These are peripheral nervous system structures."]},{"id":62,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which division includes nerves and ganglia located outside the brain and spinal cord?","choices":["Peripheral nervous system","Central nervous system","Endocrine system"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The peripheral nervous system includes neural structures outside the CNS.","The CNS consists of the brain and spinal cord.","The endocrine system is a separate regulatory system."]},{"id":63,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which part of a neuron usually receives incoming signals from other neurons?","choices":["Dendrites","Axon terminals","Myelin sheath"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Dendrites commonly receive incoming signals.","Axon terminals commonly release neurotransmitters.","Myelin insulates axons and is not the primary receiving region."]},{"id":64,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which part of a neuron generally conducts an action potential away from the cell body?","choices":["Axon","Dendrite","Myelin sheath"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The axon conducts electrical signals away from the cell body toward its terminals.","Dendrites commonly receive signals.","Myelin insulates the axon rather than conducting the signal as the neuron process itself."]},{"id":65,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which glial cell forms myelin around axons in the central nervous system?","choices":["Oligodendrocyte","Schwann cell","Astrocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Oligodendrocytes form myelin around CNS axons.","Schwann cells form myelin in the peripheral nervous system.","Astrocytes support neurons and contribute to the blood-brain barrier."]},{"id":66,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which glial cell forms myelin around peripheral nervous system axons?","choices":["Schwann cell","Oligodendrocyte","Microglia"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Schwann cells form myelin around axons in the PNS.","Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons in the CNS.","Microglia provide immune functions in the CNS."]},{"id":67,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which glial cell helps maintain the neuronal environment and contributes to the blood-brain barrier?","choices":["Astrocyte","Microglia","Schwann cell"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Astrocytes support neurons and contribute to maintaining the blood-brain barrier.","Microglia mainly perform immune and phagocytic functions.","Schwann cells myelinate peripheral axons."]},{"id":68,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which glial cells act as important immune-like phagocytic cells within the central nervous system?","choices":["Microglia","Astrocytes","Oligodendrocytes"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Microglia provide immune surveillance and phagocytic functions in the CNS.","Astrocytes support neurons and help maintain the neural environment.","Oligodendrocytes primarily produce CNS myelin."]},{"id":69,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Why does myelin increase the speed of action potential conduction along many axons?","choices":["It allows saltatory conduction between nodes","It permanently opens every sodium channel","It replaces neurotransmitters"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Myelin allows the action potential to effectively jump between nodes of Ranvier.","Myelin does not permanently open sodium channels.","Myelin is an insulating structure and is not a neurotransmitter."]},{"id":70,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"At a typical chemical synapse, neurotransmitters are released from which structure?","choices":["Axon terminal","Dendritic spine only","Myelin sheath"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The presynaptic axon terminal releases neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft.","Dendrites generally receive signals rather than release neurotransmitters at a typical synapse.","Myelin insulates axons and does not release neurotransmitters."]},{"id":71,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which event is most directly responsible for triggering neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse?","choices":["Calcium entering the presynaptic terminal","Potassium leaving the nucleus","Glucose entering the soma"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Calcium entry triggers synaptic vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release.","This is not the mechanism responsible for vesicle release.","Glucose supports metabolism but does not directly trigger vesicle fusion."]},{"id":72,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"During an action potential, which ion normally enters the neuron rapidly during the depolarization phase?","choices":["Sodium","Calcium from bone","Chloride only"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Rapid sodium entry produces the typical depolarization phase of an action potential.","Bone calcium is not responsible for typical neuronal depolarization.","Chloride does not produce the typical rapid depolarization described."]},{"id":73,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which brain region is strongly involved in coordination, balance, and fine motor control?","choices":["Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata","Hypothalamus"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The cerebellum contributes to coordination, balance, posture, and fine motor control.","The medulla contains important autonomic centers.","The hypothalamus is strongly involved in homeostasis."]},{"id":74,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which brain region contains important centers involved in regulating breathing and heart rate?","choices":["Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum","Corpus callosum"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The medulla contains important autonomic centers for cardiovascular and respiratory regulation.","The cerebellum mainly coordinates movement and balance.","The corpus callosum connects the cerebral hemispheres."]},{"id":75,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which brain region plays major roles in homeostasis, temperature regulation, and endocrine control?","choices":["Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The hypothalamus regulates homeostasis and interacts closely with the endocrine system.","The cerebellum primarily coordinates movement and balance.","The occipital lobe is primarily involved in visual processing."]},{"id":76,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"A person rapidly pulls their hand away from a painful stimulus before consciously processing the sensation. Which pathway best explains this response?","choices":["Spinal reflex","Voluntary motor pathway only","Endocrine reflex"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A spinal reflex can generate a rapid response before conscious processing is completed.","A withdrawal reflex does not require conscious initiation.","The described response is a rapid neural reflex rather than an endocrine response."]},{"id":77,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which nervous system division primarily controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement?","choices":["Somatic nervous system","Autonomic nervous system","Enteric endocrine system"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The somatic nervous system controls voluntary skeletal muscle activity.","The autonomic system primarily regulates involuntary functions.","This is not the division responsible for voluntary skeletal muscle control."]},{"id":78,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"Which division regulates many involuntary functions such as heart rate and digestion?","choices":["Autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Skeletal system"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The autonomic nervous system regulates many involuntary functions.","The somatic system controls voluntary skeletal muscle.","The skeletal system supports and protects the body but does not regulate autonomic functions."]},{"id":79,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"What is the primary role of a synapse between neurons?","choices":["Allow communication from one neuron to another","Produce red blood cells","Store calcium in bone"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A synapse provides a communication point between neurons or between a neuron and another target cell.","Blood cells are primarily produced in red bone marrow.","Bone stores minerals but this is unrelated to synaptic communication."]},{"id":80,"topic":"Nervous System","q":"If the myelin surrounding a peripheral axon is damaged, which cell type would normally be involved in forming that myelin?","choices":["Schwann cell","Oligodendrocyte","Microglia"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Schwann cells form myelin around peripheral nervous system axons.","Oligodendrocytes form myelin in the CNS.","Microglia perform immune functions in the CNS."