bulin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Thrombin converts fibrinogen into fibrin, which forms a mesh in a clot.","Albumin helps maintain plasma osmotic pressure.","Globulins include some immune proteins; they are not converted into fibrin."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"A blood sample has clotted. What is the remaining fluid, plasma without clotting factors, called?","choices":["Serum","Buffy coat","Hematocrit"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Serum is plasma after clotting factors have been removed or used in clot formation.","The buffy coat is the thin layer of white blood cells and platelets after centrifugation.","Hematocrit is the percentage of blood volume occupied by red blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What does a hematocrit measurement report?","choices":["The percentage of blood volume made up by red blood cells","The number of antibodies in plasma","The time required for blood to clot"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Hematocrit is the proportion of total blood volume occupied by red blood cells.","An antibody concentration is not what hematocrit measures.","Prothrombin time measures how long clotting takes."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"How does the biconcave shape of a red blood cell help it transport gases?","choices":["It increases surface area for gas movement","It gives the cell more room for a nucleus","It allows the cell to make platelets"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The biconcave shape increases surface area, helping gases move into and out of the cell.","Mature red blood cells lose their nucleus during development.","Platelets are cell fragments produced from megakaryocytes, not red blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which protein inside red blood cells is responsible for their color and binds respiratory gases?","choices":["Hemoglobin","Albumin","Fibrinogen"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Hemoglobin is the pigmented protein in red blood cells and carries oxygen and some carbon dioxide.","Albumin is a plasma protein.","Fibrinogen is a plasma clotting factor."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Low blood oxygen stimulates the kidneys to release which signal that promotes red blood cell production?","choices":["Erythropoietin","Histamine","Thrombin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The kidneys produce erythropoietin, which stimulates red blood cell production in red bone marrow.","Histamine is released by basophils and promotes inflammation.","Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin during clotting."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"After birth, where does hematopoiesis occur primarily?","choices":["Red bone marrow","The plasma","The heart valves"],"answer":0,"explanations":["After birth, blood cell production occurs primarily in red bone marrow, with some white cells produced in lymphatic tissues.","Plasma is the liquid matrix of blood and is not the main site of blood cell production.","Heart valves direct blood flow and do not produce blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which group contains all three types of granulocytes?","choices":["Neutrophils, basophils, and eosinophils","Lymphocytes, monocytes, and platelets","Erythrocytes, lymphocytes, and monocytes"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The three granulocytes are neutrophils, basophils, and eosinophils.","Lymphocytes and monocytes are agranulocytes, and platelets are cell fragments.","Erythrocytes are red blood cells; lymphocytes and monocytes are agranulocytes."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which pair consists of agranulocytes?","choices":["Lymphocytes and monocytes","Neutrophils and eosinophils","Basophils and platelets"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The two agranulocyte types are lymphocytes and monocytes.","Neutrophils and eosinophils are granulocytes.","Basophils are granulocytes, and platelets are not white blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which white blood cell is the most common and commonly phagocytizes microorganisms?","choices":["Neutrophil","Basophil","Eosinophil"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Neutrophils are the most common leukocytes and phagocytize microorganisms and other foreign substances.","Basophils release histamine and heparin and promote inflammation.","Eosinophils are associated with allergic responses and defense against parasitic worms."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which white blood cell releases histamine and heparin?","choices":["Basophil","Neutrophil","Monocyte"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Basophils release histamine, which promotes inflammation, and heparin, which helps prevent clot formation.","Neutrophils primarily phagocytize microorganisms.","Monocytes can enter tissues and become macrophages."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"An increased eosinophil count is most consistent with which finding from the chapter?","choices":["Allergic responses or parasitic worm infection","A platelet plug forming in a small vessel","Red blood cell production in marrow"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Eosinophils participate in allergic and inflammatory responses and help destroy certain worm parasites.","Platelets form plugs at vessel injuries; eosinophils do not make the plug.","Red blood cell production is stimulated by erythropoietin in red bone marrow."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which white blood cells are involved in antibody production and other immune responses?","choices":["Lymphocytes","Erythrocytes","Platelets"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Lymphocytes participate in antibody production and many other immune activities.","Erythrocytes transport respiratory gases.","Platelets help prevent blood loss."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What can a monocyte become after it leaves the blood and enters tissues?","choices":["A macrophage that phagocytizes debris and can present antigens","A red blood cell that carries oxygen","A platelet that releases hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Monocytes enlarge into macrophages in tissues, where they ingest debris and can activate lymphocytes by presenting processed foreign material.","Monocytes do not become red blood cells after entering tissues.","Platelets are fragments formed from megakaryocytes, not monocytes."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"A small tear occurs in a blood vessel. Which response can seal the break with an accumulation of platelets?","choices":["Platelet plug formation","Fibrinolysis","Erythropoiesis"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A platelet plug can seal a small break in a vessel and help maintain vessel integrity.","Fibrinolysis breaks down a clot after it has served its function.","Erythropoiesis is the production of red blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Why is clotting needed in addition to vessel constriction and a platelet plug after a severe vessel injury?","choices":["It forms a fibrin network that stabilizes the repair","It dissolves all platelets immediately","It turns red blood cells into white blood cells"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Coagulation forms fibrin fibers that trap cells and platelets, stabilizing a clot over a larger injury.","Clotting stabilizes a clot; it does not immediately dissolve all platelets.","Red and white blood cells are distinct formed-element lineages."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"In the clotting sequence, what is formed after clotting factors are activated in a cascade?","choices":["Prothrombinase","Plasmin","Albumin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The cascade of activated clotting factors produces prothrombinase, also called prothrombin activator.","Plasmin breaks down fibrin during fibrinolysis.","Albumin is a plasma protein and is not the product of the clotting-factor cascade."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What does prothrombinase do in the clotting process?","choices":["Converts prothrombin into thrombin","Converts fibrin into fibrinogen","Breaks down a clot into plasma"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Prothrombinase activates prothrombin, converting it to thrombin.","The next clotting step converts fibrinogen into fibrin; fibrin is not converted back this way.","Clot breakdown is fibrinolysis, involving plasmin."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which clotting protein converts fibrinogen into fibrin?","choices":["Thrombin","Albumin","Hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen into threadlike fibrin fibers.","Albumin helps maintain plasma osmotic pressure.","Hemoglobin is the respiratory-gas carrying protein inside red blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which substances in blood help prevent inappropriate clot formation?","choices":["Antithrombin and heparin","Fibrin and thrombin","Hemoglobin and albumin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Antithrombin and heparin are anticoagulants that help prevent clot formation.","Fibrin and thrombin participate in forming a clot.","Hemoglobin transports gases and albumin is a plasma protein; they are not the anticoagulants named in the chapter."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What is fibrinolysis?","choices":["Breakdown of a clot by plasmin","Formation of a platelet plug by albumin","Production of red cells by erythropoietin"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Fibrinolysis is clot breakdown, accomplished by the enzyme plasmin.","A platelet plug is formed by platelets; albumin is not its main structural component.","Erythropoietin stimulates red blood cell production, not clot formation."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What determines a person's ABO blood group?","choices":["Antigens on the surface of red blood cells","The amount of albumin in plasma","The number of platelets in the buffy coat"],"answer":0,"explanations":["ABO groups are determined by which A and B antigens are present on red blood cell surfaces.","Albumin concentration does not determine ABO type.","Platelet number does not determine ABO type."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which antigen and antibody pattern is expected for type A blood?","choices":["A antigen and anti-B antibodies","B antigen and anti-A antibodies","A and B antigens with both anti-A and anti-B antibodies"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Type A blood has A antigens on red cells and anti-B antibodies in plasma.","That pattern describes type B blood.","Type AB blood has both antigens and neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which antigen and antibody pattern is expected for type AB blood?","choices":["A and B antigens, with neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies","No A or B antigens, with both anti-A and anti-B antibodies","A antigen with anti-B antibodies"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Type AB red blood cells carry both A and B antigens; plasma has neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies.","That pattern describes type O blood.","That pattern describes type A blood."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which antigen and antibody pattern is expected for type O blood?","choices":["Neither A nor B antigens, with both anti-A and anti-B antibodies","Both A and B antigens, with no anti-A or anti-B antibodies","B antigen with anti-A antibodies"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Type O red blood cells have neither A nor B antigens, and plasma has both anti-A and anti-B antibodies.","That pattern describes type AB blood.","That pattern describes type B blood."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What can happen when donor and recipient ABO blood groups are mismatched?","choices":["Antibody binding can cause agglutination or hemolysis of red blood cells","The blood automatically forms more albumin","The red blood cells become platelets"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A transfusion mismatch can cause antibodies to bind red-cell antigens, resulting in agglutination or hemolysis.","Albumin production is not the response to an ABO mismatch.","Red blood cells do not transform into platelets."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What does a blood crossmatch test?","choices":["Whether donor and recipient blood produce an agglutination reaction","The percentage of red cells in whole blood","How many granulocytes are present"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A crossmatch checks for agglutination reactions between donor and recipient blood.","The percentage of blood volume made up of red cells is the hematocrit.","A differential white blood count measures the proportions of leukocyte types."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"Which set of measurements is included in a complete blood count (CBC) as described in the chapter?","choices":["Red blood cell count, hemoglobin, hematocrit, and white blood cell count","Blood type, plasma albumin, and blood glucose only","Heart rate, blood pressure, and oxygen saturation"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A CBC includes red-cell count, hemoglobin and hematocrit measurements, and white-cell count.","These are not the components listed for a CBC in the chapter.","These are vital-sign measures, not the CBC components listed in the chapter."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What does a differential white blood count measure?","choices":["The percentage of each of the five white blood cell types","The total volume of plasma in the body","The time until a blood clot starts to form"],"answer":0,"explanations":["A differential count reports the relative proportions of neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils.","Plasma volume is not what a differential white count measures.","Prothrombin time measures how long blood takes to begin clotting."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"A bacterial infection is accompanied by leukocytosis. Which cell increase commonly contributes to this finding?","choices":["Neutrophils","Eosinophils only","Platelets"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Bacterial infections often stimulate an increase in neutrophils, contributing to leukocytosis.","Eosinophils are especially associated with allergic responses and parasitic worms.","Platelets are cell fragments involved in preventing blood loss, not leukocytes."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"A patient has a greatly reduced platelet count and persistent small-vessel bleeding. Which term best describes the platelet finding?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia","Leukocytosis","Erythrocytosis"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Thrombocytopenia is a greatly reduced platelet count and can cause chronic bleeding through small vessels.","Leukocytosis is an abnormally high white blood cell count.","Erythrocytosis is an overabundance of red blood cells."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular & Blood","q":"What does prothrombin time assess, and how is the result commonly standardized?","choices":["How long clotting takes to begin; it is reported as an INR","The percentage of red cells; it is reported as a hematocrit","The number of white cells; it is reported as a differential"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Prothrombin time measures how long blood takes to start clotting and is reported as the International Normalized Ratio.","Hematocrit reports the fraction of blood volume made up of red blood cells.","A differential reports the proportions of the different white blood cell types."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular System","q":"Which valve lies between the right atrium and right ventricle?","choices":["Tricuspid valve","Mitral valve","Aortic valve"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, between the right atrium and right ventricle.","The mitral valve is on the left side, between the left atrium and left ventricle.","The aortic valve lies between the left ventricle and the aorta."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular System","q":"Which valve guards the opening from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk?","choices":["Pulmonary semilunar valve","Aortic semilunar valve","Tricuspid valve"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The pulmonary valve is at the right ventricular outflow into the pulmonary trunk.","The aortic valve guards the outflow from the left ventricle into the aorta.","The tricuspid valve is between the right atrium and right ventricle."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular System","q":"The mitral, or bicuspid, valve is located between which chambers?","choices":["Left atrium and left ventricle","Right atrium and right ventricle","Right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The mitral (bicuspid) valve is the left atrioventricular valve, between the left atrium and left ventricle.","The tricuspid valve is between the right atrium and right ventricle.","The pulmonary semilunar valve is between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular System","q":"Which valve allows blood to leave the left ventricle for the aorta?","choices":["Aortic semilunar valve","Pulmonary semilunar valve","Mitral valve"],"answer":0,"explanations":["The aortic valve is between the left ventricle and aorta and prevents backflow into the ventricle.","The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle toward the lungs.","The mitral valve is between the left atrium and left ventricle."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular System","q":"Which pairing correctly groups the heart valves by location?","choices":["Atrioventricular: tricuspid and mitral; semilunar: pulmonary and aortic","Atrioventricular: pulmonary and aortic; semilunar: tricuspid and mitral","All four valves are atrioventricular valves"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Tricuspid and mitral valves sit between atria and ventricles; pulmonary and aortic valves sit at ventricular outflow openings.","This reverses the two groups.","Only the tricuspid and mitral valves are atrioventricular valves."]},{"topic":"Cardiovascular System","q":"Which sequence follows blood through the heart and pulmonary circuit in the correct order?","choices":["Right atrium → tricuspid → right ventricle → pulmonary valve → lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → mitral → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta","Right atrium → mitral → left ventricle → aortic valve → lungs → right ventricle → pulmonary valve → aorta","Left atrium → tricuspid → right ventricle → aorta → lungs → pulmonary veins → left ventricle"],"answer":0,"explanations":["Blood returns to the right atrium, passes through the right heart to the lungs, returns to the left atrium, and leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve.","This mixes right- and left-sided structures and sends blood through the aorta before the pulmonary circuit.","This begins on the wrong side and places the aorta before pulmonary oxygenation."]}]];
